1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,640
Well done, well done.

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:05,920
You've reached the end of functions and this is a really important module.

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:10,920
This is where we've learned to create these little, these little boxes that encapsulate code.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,360
Code that can be used over and over again.

5
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,960
So as we said functions there are lots of different types of functions.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,400
And they fit into categories broader categories called modules.

7
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:25,320
And so you can have modules that have to do with math functions as we've seen, or modules that have

8
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:31,480
to do with graphic functions, modules that handle manipulation of lists, etc., all different types

9
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:32,320
of functions.

10
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,960
And not only did we look at them, we looked at how you can use them and you've been using them the

11
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:37,760
whole time, whether you knew it or not.

12
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:42,920
Simple functions like print, but when you're using them, or more exciting functions like random,

13
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:47,360
which we want to use in our game that we're going to create shortly, those functions always require

14
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:48,520
you to pass parameters.

15
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:52,840
Very important that you pass the correct number of parameters in order to call the function.

16
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:54,440
And then the function will return something.

17
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:56,120
That's the whole reason they're there.

18
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:57,840
They're not just a black box that does nothing.

19
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:01,080
They normally return a value and we can work with that value.

20
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:04,120
And so we moved on and showed you how you could create your own function.

21
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:08,160
We created a function that looked at the days of the week and whether something was a weekend.

22
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:13,080
And what was exciting about that is you could use that function to test if a certain day of the week

23
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:14,880
was the weekend or not.

24
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:20,840
So functions are a very powerful, and they are something that you're going to use increasingly.

25
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:22,720
So they're not difficult to use.

26
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:25,600
It's all about you're putting your code into these little packages.

27
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:30,280
And then once they're packaged and tested, you know that you can continue to easily use it all over

28
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:31,120
your program.

29
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:34,080
So these are very good habits that you need to develop.

30
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:35,200
And we've looked at a couple of them.

31
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,240
We've looked at layout how you must keep your indentation proper.

32
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:40,600
We've looked at including comments.

33
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:47,560
And another very important one is keeping the cohesiveness of your modules and of your coding by putting

34
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:49,040
together functions.

35
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:49,560
So well done.

36
